The module is rooted at go/: go.mod there declares `module github.com/voxgig-sdk/demo-sdk/go` (from goModule: main.kit.target.go.module.path, else <repo host>/<repo path>/go) with no external requires (struct is vendored at utility/struct). The root package is in go/ itself, so a program must be `package main` in its own directory (e.g. go/cmd/demo/main.go; `go run file.go` naming one file also works). (a) from go/: `package main
import (
  "fmt"
  sdk "github.com/voxgig-sdk/demo-sdk/go"
)
func main() { client := sdk.NewDemoSDK(map[string]any{...}); ... }` run with `go run ./cmd/demo` - the import resolves to the main module itself. (b) from the repository root: there is no go.mod or go.work at the root (go.work is even gitignored), so the same file is outside any module and the import would be fetched from the network (fails until published); it needs its own go.mod with `require github.com/voxgig-sdk/demo-sdk/go v0.0.0` + `replace github.com/voxgig-sdk/demo-sdk/go => ./go`, or `go run -C go ./cmd/demo`. The old root README quick start printed `import sdk "github.com/voxgig-sdk/demo-sdk/go"` followed by bare statements (`client := sdk.NewDemoSDK(...)`, `fmt.Println(...)`, `os.Getenv`) with no `package main`, no `func main`, and no fmt/os imports: it does not compile anywhere as printed, and from the root it would also lack a module.
